{"bill":{"id":"119-sjres-47","congress":119,"billType":"sjres","billNumber":"47","title":"A joint resolution providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"California State Motor Vehicle and Engine and Nonroad Engine Pollution Control Standards; The 'Omnibus' Low NOx Regulation; Waiver of Preemption; Notice of Decision\".","introducedDate":"2025-04-04","latestActionDate":"2025-04-04","latestActionText":"Read twice and referred to the Committee on Environment and Public Works.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Air quality","California","Climate change and greenhouse gases","Congressional oversight","Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)","Federal preemption","Hybrid, electric, and advanced technology vehicles","Motor vehicles"],"sponsorBioguideId":"M001190","cosponsorBioguideIds":["C001047","F000463","L000575","J000312","C000880","S001198","C001096","R000618","K000393","T000278","L000577","L000571","M000934"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution nullifies the Environmental Protection Agency notice titled California State Motor Vehicle and Engine and Nonroad Engine Pollution Control Standards; The ‘Omnibus’ Low NOX Regulation; Waiver of Preemption; Notice of Decision (90 Fed. Reg. 643) and published on January 6, 2025. Among other elements, the notice grants the California Air Resources Board request for a waiver for its Heavy-Duty Vehicle and Engine Omnibus Low NOX regulations. (Under the Clean Air Act, California may seek waivers of the preemption prohibiting states from enacting certain emission standards.)","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-joint-resolution/47","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/sjres/BILLSTATUS-119sjres47.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:26.187Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-sjres-47","plainSummary":"This joint resolution would overturn an Environmental Protection Agency decision that allowed California to enforce stricter pollution standards for heavy-duty trucks and engines than federal law normally permits. The EPA had granted California a waiver to implement its \"Omnibus Low NOx\" regulation, which sets tougher limits on nitrogen oxide emissions from vehicles. If this resolution passes, that waiver would be nullified and California would be blocked from enforcing those stricter standards.","industries":[{"industry":"transport","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly affects heavy-duty trucking by determining whether stricter California emission standards apply to commercial vehicles and engines."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill affects manufacturers of heavy-duty vehicles and engines by determining whether they must comply with California's stricter NOx emission requirements."},{"industry":"energy-fossil","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Stricter emission standards for heavy-duty vehicles could affect demand for diesel fuel and related petroleum products."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"8d3c54e5d054c7322b479aa3","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:49:30.991Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would cancel the EPA notice titled California State Motor Vehicle and Engine and Nonroad Engine Pollution Control Standards; The ‘Omnibus’ Low NOX Regulation; Waiver of Preemption; Notice of Decision (90 Fed. Reg.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who drives. People living near a road, a plant or a port.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in driving, and what a car must have. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is furt